{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And it bred worms and rotted</b> — this is an inverted verse, for first it rotted and afterward bred worms, as it says (Exodus 16:24) \"and it did not rot, nor was there a worm in it.\" And so is the way of everything that breeds worms. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 16:20). Now, if the manna bred worms by natural course, like other things that breed worms, the matter would be so. But since this bred worms by way of miracle, it is possible that it raised up worms first, and there is no need for us to invert the verse. Moreover, the verse that says <b>and it did not rot, nor was there a worm in it</b>, proves this very point: for if it did not raise up worms until after it had first turned foul, when it says \"and it did not rot,\" it would already have assured us that there was no worm in it — so why then does it add afterward, \"nor was there a worm in it\"? But if it raised up worms first, according to the plain sense of the verse, it was necessary to say that this one did not rot and also no worm arose in it at all. Furthermore, even among things that breed worms naturally, only the moist ones among them rot, but the dry ones raise up worms and do not rot at all — like worm-eaten wood and fruits that raise up worms in their unripe stage or afterward. And Scripture relates that this one also rotted by miracle. And in Shemot Rabbah (25:10:14) they said: Now is there any thing that first produces worms and afterward rots? Rather, the Holy One, blessed be He, sought to show their deeds to created beings — so that they would not smell its odor in the evening and throw it away; for it would produce all night long row after row of worms. Immediately, <b>and Moses was angry with them</b>."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:09:21.292683112Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}